  {# 6. The story #}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>The story of AZERTY Global</h2>

      <div class="prose presse-recit">
        <p>
          In late 2015, the French Ministry of Culture asked AFNOR, the French standards body, to create a new keyboard standard.
          In January 2016, it published the report
          <a href="https://www.culture.gouv.fr/thematiques/langue-francaise-et-langues-de-france/agir-pour-les-langues/innover-dans-le-domaine-des-langues-et-du-numerique/accompagner-les-technologies-de-la-langue-et-la-normalisation/Vers-une-norme-francaise-pour-les-claviers-informatiques" lang="fr">«&nbsp;Vers une norme française pour les claviers informatiques&nbsp;»</a>
          (“Towards a French standard for computer keyboards”), which establishes that <strong>it is impossible to type French correctly on an AZERTY keyboard</strong>.
          No accented capitals in one press, no French quotation marks, no œ…
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2019, the <a href="https://norme-azerty.fr" hreflang="fr">NF Z71-300 standard<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> was published. But this official solution
          moves <strong>31&nbsp;characters</strong> compared with traditional Windows AZERTY,
          only partly meets developers’ needs, and <strong>struggles to catch on</strong>:
          very few compatible keyboards are on sale and adoption remains marginal.
        </p>
        <picture class="presse-recit__portrait">
          <source srcset="/images/antoine-clavier-cadre-300.webp" type="image/webp">
          <img src="/images/antoine-clavier-cadre-300.jpg" alt="Antoine Olivier holding a keyboard with the AZERTY Global logo" width="152" height="152" loading="lazy">
        </picture>
        <p>
          Without waiting for the standard, <strong>Antoine Olivier</strong>, then a student in a preparatory class
          for France’s <em>grandes écoles</em>, co-founded AZERTY Global in 2017 with Robert Hodge, a retired
          American engineer, and has developed it since 2018. A keen enthusiast of ergonomic keyboards
          and keyboard layouts, he had had enough of the flaws of traditional AZERTY.
          He has kept the project moving ever since.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal: <strong>a layout based on traditional Windows AZERTY</strong>,
          to ease the transition, but fixing its major flaws and adding
          many characters, to type French perfectly and nearly 300&nbsp;languages in all.
          AZERTY Global has been through several versions since 2017.
          <strong>The 2026 version is by far the most polished</strong>,
          the result of continuous improvement.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="bloc-amcf presse-encadre" aria-label="Not to be confused with the AFNOR AZERTY">
        <p><strong>Not to be confused with the AFNOR AZERTY</strong></p>
        <p>
          AZERTY Global is an <strong>independent project</strong>, distinct from the AFNOR standard (NF Z71-300, 2019).
          The two layouts share some goals but differ in approach. AZERTY Global
          favors a <strong>gentle transition</strong> from traditional Windows AZERTY.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/en/comparison">Compare AZERTY Global and the AFNOR standard</a></p>
      </aside>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>The foundations of the project</h3>
        <ul class="presse-faits">
          <li><strong><a href="https://www.dispoclavier.com/afnor/enq2017/comm/public/tout/" hreflang="fr">AFNOR public survey of June 2017<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a></strong>: feedback from the public on the
            problems of the AZERTY keyboard shaped the development priorities.</li>
          <li><strong>Study of foreign layouts</strong>: inspiration from international good practice,
            such as dead keys on the number row (Hungarian layout) or Smart Caps Lock
            (Spanish QWERTY).</li>
          <li><strong>Intuition and daily practice</strong>: daily use and continuous improvement of
            the layout by Antoine Olivier, who has developed it since 2018.</li>
          <li><strong>Community feedback</strong>: feedback from the first users shaped the successive
            versions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

